WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.950 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  this is luke krafka's passion  music and live performance the  professional cellist from brooklyn  often plays in concerts with a changing  cast of musicians  who are usually paid in cash and he says  mobile payment apps have made that task  a lot simpler paypal venmo cash up yep  there's no paper involved there's no  checks involved there's no waiting you  get paid  right away goes to your bank account  it's actually made it's made life a lot  easier  he also plays at weddings in october  2019  a new customer reached out to hire him  it was gearing up to be  a normal you know wedding and i had  reached out to my friends they were good  for the gig  the client told him he'd send him a  check roughly a thousand dollars for  krafka  and another 950 to pay for the client's  sound man who he asked krafka to then  pay  through a mobile payment app and i said  look if you send me the money  that's no problem that's fine mobile  payment apps allow users to pay  others immediately through bank accounts  or credit cards connected to the app  it's easy simply hit a few buttons on  the app on your phone  and your friend or contact gets the  money right away  usually a good thing but not always as  krafko was about to find  out he deposited the wedding client's  check for nineteen hundred  sixty dollars i checked you know the  following day  and the full amount was in my bank  account  then as requested he sent the client  sound man 950 dollars  through the payment app zell i believe  it was the following day  that check bounced  and when that happened then i  slowly started to put all of this  together  sure enough the people who supposedly  hired him stopped answering his calls  and he was out 950 dollars and i said  look i think i've been scammed  krafka and many others this report from  the u.s public interest research group  or perg looked at over 9 000  complaints to federal regulators about  digital wallets and payment apps  including paypal venmo cash app and zell  they found over 2 700 were filed between  january and april of this year  up over 300 percent from the same period  last year  with many losing money to scams or fraud  if it's a scammer it is not coming back  perks ed merzwinski says compounding the  problems  is that unlike with credit or even debit  cards app  transactions are instantaneous there are  no rules that protect you  very well at all when your money goes  out of your account and into somebody  else's account  especially if you initiated the  transaction and sent the money  a lesson krafka learned when he reached  out to his bank to get his money back  they told him there was nothing they  could do i was surprised that there was  no recourse and that  once it's done it's done and there's  nothing you  can do

